/// Something outside is read on a schedule, and the row remembers how that
/// went.
///
/// ─── Inline or a run log, and both are right ──────────────────────────────
///
/// portal `Follow` (a feed: `etag`, `lastModified`, `failures`, `error`,
/// `polledAt`, `nextPollAt`) and portal `Page` (link health: `linkFailures`,
/// `checkedAt`, `goneAt`) keep the state ON the thing being polled. transit
/// `Source` keeps a little (`syncedAt`, `state Json`) and moves the rest into
/// a `SyncRun` row per attempt with a `Reject` row per bad record. Outside
/// the repo openmrp has both shapes (`HubspotSyncJob` plus a per-record
/// `SyncRecord`, and `EdiRun`), documenso three, discourse six.
///
/// The split is by what a person needs to read. A feed reader asks "is this
/// feed alive and when do we try again" — one row answers it. An operator
/// loading a customer's CSV asks "what happened on Tuesday's run, and which
/// 14 lines were refused" — that is history, and history is rows. This trait
/// is the inline half. The run log is a model (`Run`, with a `status` enum
/// and `@@transitions` the engine moves `@system`) and transit's `SyncRun` is
/// the instance to derive it from when it is written.
///
/// ─── Every column is `@system` ────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A poller's state is written by the poller. A user who could set
/// `nextPollAt` could make a feed hammer its origin; one who could zero
/// `failures` could resurrect a dead source past its own back-off. The host
/// exposes what a person may change (`paused`, `keywords`, the URL itself)
/// under its own columns, and those are the ones a form shows.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `status` enum.** `failures` plus `error` plus `nextPollAt` IS the
/// state: zero failures and a next time is healthy, failures with a next
/// time is backing off, failures with no next time is given up. An enum
/// beside those is a second copy that drifts the first time a hook forgets
/// it. portal's `FollowState { active gone }` is the honest exception — it
/// records a human decision to stop, which no counter can express — and a
/// host that needs that word adds it.
///
/// **No `interval`.** How often is the schedule's business, usually a cron
/// string on the host or a job the engine runs; the row only records when it
/// next expects to be visited.
///
/// **No fetched content.** `Follow` keeps the posts in `Post`; `Page` keeps
/// the capture in its own columns; the trait is only the bookkeeping around
/// a fetch, never its result.
trait Poller {
  /// The last attempt, succeeded or not.
  polledAt   DateTime? @system

  /// When the engine should look again. Null means never: the source is
  /// paused, gone, or has exhausted its back-off. This is the column the
  /// scheduler's query reads, so it is the one indexed.
  nextPollAt DateTime? @system

  /// Consecutive failures since the last success; reset to zero by a
  /// success. The back-off is a function of this number, and so is giving up.
  failures   Int       @system @default(0)

  /// The last failure's message, kept until the next success clears it, so
  /// an operator sees why without reading a log.
  error      String?   @system

  /// Conditional-request validators from the last successful fetch, sent back
  /// as `If-None-Match` / `If-Modified-Since` so an unchanged source costs a
  /// 304. Both nullable: not every source speaks them.
  etag         String? @system
  lastModified String? @system

  /// "Everything due now" — the one query a scheduler runs, every tick.
  @@index([nextPollAt])
}
